  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nNighttime satellite currently shows patchy low clouds caught in the \nflow of a weak coastal eddy drifting into coastal Orange County. \nClouds should eventually fill in over coastal areas overnight with \ngreater inland coverage to the western valleys (and patchy fog) in \nthe following nights.\n\nThe ridge that spiked coastal temperatures over the past week \ncontinues to move inland into the south central US. This has \nincreased the depth of our marine layer influence to reach into the \ninterior valleys with overnight humidities reaching 80-100% in these \nlower areas for the next few nights. \n\nToday's temperatures will be similar to yesterday's: upper 70s near \nthe beaches, 80s in coastal areas, low 90s inland, mid-to-upper 90s \nin the IE and high desert and 100s in the lower deserts. \n\nSunday's temperatures fall another 3-5 degrees as the trough to the \nnorth moving through the PacNW continues to displace the remaining \nridge influence from SoCal, restoring near average max and min \ntemperatures region-wide.\n\nThere is moderate confidence that cooling continues into Tuesday \ngiven the northern trough looks to expand in length and maintain \nits influence as it moves eastward. Temperatures early week could \nbe a few degrees below average with highs back to the mid-70s at \nthe coast, only reaching to around 90 degrees in the interior \nvalleys/IE/high desert and around 100-102 degrees in the lower \ndeserts. Low temperatures in the interior valleys Tuesday and \nWednesday mornings could drop into the mid-50s, offering \nsignificantly cooler nighttime air as compared to last week. \nElsewhere in the coastal basin, lows will drop into the lower 60s.\n\nAlthough model uncertainty is higher Wednesday onward, both the GFS \nand ECMWF show the ridge in the Southern Plains expanding westward, \ninitiating another warming trend to some degree. Inland temperatures \nmay increase once again another 5-15 degrees while there is greater \nuncertainty for coastal areas as this will depend on the placement \nof the peak ridge axis.",
